    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Let me summarize what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the same first and last name (case-insensitively), computed before the new owner is saved, persisted, and returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nChanges, each kept to a single responsibility:\n\n1. **`Namesakes.java`** (new helper, package-private) \u2014 canonical name matching: two owners are namesakes when first and last name match after trim/whitespace-collapse/lower-case. Mirrors the existing `Households` / `CustomerCode` helper style so the comparison logic lives in one place.\n\n2. **`CountNamesakes.java`** (new function) \u2014 reads the built `@Val Owner` and the `OwnerRepository`, counts existing owners matching via `Namesakes.matches`, and stores it with `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`. Because it runs before `save`, the count excludes the new owner.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `countNamesakes` into the pipeline between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbours.\n\n4. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persisted `namesakeCount` (`namesake_count` column) field with getter/setter and a `toString` entry.\n\n5. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"namesakeCount\", ignore = true)` to `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (consistent with the existing `customerCode`/`householdId` ignores); the `Owner \u2192 OwnerDto` mapping picks the field up by name automatically.\n\n6. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` response schema.\n\n7. **schema.sql** (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column; the test profile uses `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the schema script.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` passes, and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).",
